# The reviewer column: a second agent checks the first one's answers, and a
# formula counts the disagreements. Column B answers each question from the
# document; column C asks a different agent whether B's answer is supported
# by the passage B cited, answering only agree or disagree; column D says
# why, in one line, when they disagree. `disagreements` is the number to
# watch. Fill B:D down over your documents, Run Selection, and read D first.
#
# Why two agents: a model checking its own answer tends to repeat its mistakes. A
# reviewer on its own context, told only to agree or disagree, is the
# cheapest independent check a table can carry, and its verdicts are cells
# a formula can count.
instructions = Answer from the document only. If the document is silent, answer Not found. Be terse: a value, a date, yes/no, or one sentence.
A1 = document
B1 = Can either party assign the agreement without the other's consent?
C1 = Reviewer: is B's answer supported by the document? (agree / disagree)
D1 = Reviewer: if you disagree, why, in one line
A2 = contracts/acme-msa.md
A3 = contracts/globex-services.md
B2 = @claude('{instructions} Document: {$A2}. Question: {B$1}')
C2 = @codex("You are reviewing another agent's answer. Document: {$A2}. Question: {B$1}. Their answer: {B2}. Read the document yourself and decide whether their answer is what the document supports. Answer with exactly one word: agree or disagree.")
D2 = @codex('Another agent read document {$A2} and answered the question "{B$1}" with: {B2}. A reviewer said {C2}. If the reviewer disagreed, say in one line what the document actually supports and where; if it agreed, answer Not found.')
disagreements = = countif({C2:C3}, 'disagree')
reviewed = = count({C2:C3}) & ' of ' & count({A2:A3}) & ' reviewed'
@answer_type B2 B3 = yesno
@answer_type C2 C3 = choice:agree|disagree
